XPilot NG builds an SDL2/OpenGL client and a dedicated server.

REQUIREMENTS

The common build requirements are:

    A C99 compiler
    GNU Make
    pkg-config
    expat
    zlib

The SDL client additionally requires:

    SDL2 version 2.0.9 or newer
    SDL2_ttf version 2.0.12 or newer
    SDL2_image version 2.0.0 or newer
    OpenGL 3.3 core profile

Sound support is optional and requires OpenAL and freealut.


INSTALLATION FROM SOURCE

1. Review the available configuration options and configure the build:

        ./configure --help
        ./configure

   To build the server without the SDL client, use:

        ./configure --disable-sdl-client

2. Build and run the complete test suite:

        make
        make check

   Maintainers can validate both the normal and server-only configurations,
   installation, distribution archive, and end-to-end TCP behavior with:

        ./tests/run-full-suite.sh

3. Install the selected programs, data, and manual pages:

        make install


DIRECT TCP PLAY

Start a local server and client in separate terminals:

    xpilot-ng-server
    xpilot-ng-sdl

With no server argument, the SDL client connects directly to 127.0.0.1. To
connect to another host, pass exactly one server name or address:

    xpilot-ng-sdl game.example.org

Gameplay and server-control requests use one fixed TCP listener. Both
programs default to TCP port 15345; select another matching port with
`-port`:

    xpilot-ng-server -port 15346
    xpilot-ng-sdl -port 15346 game.example.org

There is no built-in lobby, LAN discovery, or metaserver listing. Server
addresses must be supplied directly or managed outside these programs. A
firewall or NAT gateway only needs to allow or forward the selected TCP port.
The client's optional `clientPortStart` and `clientPortEnd` settings restrict
its TCP source-port range; the server no longer allocates per-player ports.


SERVER CONTROL

Status and the interactive control interface use short-lived TCP connections
to the same server and port:

    xpilot-ng-sdl -status game.example.org
    xpilot-ng-sdl -text 127.0.0.1
    xpilot-ng-sdl -shutdown "maintenance" 127.0.0.1

Status and visible-option queries are public. Commands that change server
state are accepted only from an IPv4 loopback connection when the username
claimed by the client exactly matches the operating-system account that owns
the server process. This is a local administrative boundary, not
cryptographic authentication.


TRANSPORT SCOPE

The application opens no UDP sockets. Existing XPilot gameplay payloads are
carried in ordered, length-framed TCP records. WebSocket, TLS, HTTP asset
delivery, and a browser/WebAssembly runtime are not provided by this step.
See doc/TCP_GAMEPLAY_TRANSPORT.md for the wire format, lifecycle, tradeoffs,
and future WebSocket boundary.

See the manual pages and the files in the doc directory for gameplay and map
configuration information.
